An iOS app that runs is in front.

The game starts, reaches its frame loop, and then does nothing: alive for four
minutes on three percent of a core, the log stopped after renderer setup, the
screen showing the renderer's clear colour.

Frame() decides whether to do anything at all from

    const bool needUpdate{g_app_has_focus || g_NetClient || !g_PauseOnFocusLoss};

and with pauseonfocusloss on (the default) a frame without focus is ten
milliseconds of sleep and no rendering. Renderer.cpp asks the same of
g_app_minimized before drawing at all.

SDL reports both states on iOS -- and on iOS neither can be true while the code
is running to see it: an app that is not frontmost is suspended by the system.
So they are ignored there. On the desktop nothing changes.

Found by measuring rather than guessing: the process was alive the whole time
and used 8 seconds of CPU in 240, which is neither a crash nor slowness but a
sleep.

--- source/ps/Globals.cpp
+++ source/ps/Globals.cpp
@@ -50,7 +50,9 @@
 		switch(ev->ev.window.event)
 		{
 		case SDL_WINDOWEVENT_MINIMIZED:
+#if !OS_IOS
 			g_app_minimized = true;
+#endif
 			break;
 		case SDL_WINDOWEVENT_EXPOSED:
 		case SDL_WINDOWEVENT_RESTORED:
@@ -60,7 +62,16 @@
 			g_app_has_focus = true;
 			break;
 		case SDL_WINDOWEVENT_FOCUS_LOST:
+#if !OS_IOS
+			// On iOS an app that is not frontmost does not run: the system
+			// suspends it. So "lost focus" and "minimized" are states this
+			// code can never actually observe from the inside -- but SDL does
+			// report them, and acting on them puts every frame into the
+			// non-focus path in main.cpp: ten milliseconds of sleep, no
+			// rendering. That looks exactly like a hung game and measures like
+			// one, at three percent of a core with nothing on screen.
 			g_app_has_focus = false;
+#endif
 			break;
 		case SDL_WINDOWEVENT_ENTER:
 			g_mouse_active = true;
